  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n- The goal is to minimize the maximum overlap integral by finding a periodic step function on [0,2] with integral 1.\n- We leverage the periodic boundary condition and discrete samples to approximate the integral via linear correlation.\n- The optimization is nonconvex due to the nature of the overlap integral. We use local search around the parent solution while experimenting with different resolutions.\n- Key limitation: directly optimizing the continuous problem is intractable, so we rely on discrete representations and local optimization.\n- Current record is 0.3808, we seek a lower value by improving the parent solution and experimenting with higher resolutions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Input: n_points (number of samples), dx = 2.0 / n_points\n- Variables: h_values in [0,1] for each sample, with sum(h_values) * dx = 1\n- Objective: minimize the maximum correlation value between h and (1-h) computed with mode='full' and scaled by dx\n- Constraint: sum(h_values) = n_points/2 exactly and 0 <= h_values[i] <= 1 for all i\n- Periodicity implied in the integral definition over real shifts\n- Resolution flexibility: transfer the parent's feasibility preservation technique to new resolutions\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Start with n_points around the parent's resolution (47 samples) and nearby (e.g., 50, 46)\n- For each n_points:\n   - Initialize h_values using the parent solution for nearby resolutions or uniform sampling for others\n   - Use SLSQP method from scipy.optimize with box constraints [0,1] and equality constraint sum(h)=n_points/2\n   - Handle unfeasible candidates by projection onto the constraint set\n   - Track best candidate across all resolutions\n- Use time budget enforcement for each optimization run\n- Repair mechanisms:\n   - For box constraints: clamp values to [0,1]\n   - For sum constraint: project uniformly (subtract mean) and rebalance by adding small random perturbations\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initialization:\n   - For n_points=47: use parent solution if available or truncation/extension\n   - For other n_points: start from uniform sampling [0.5] or parent-extended projection\n- Objective computation:\n   - Use np.correlate with mode='full' to approximate the maximum overlap\n   - dx = 2.0 / n_points used in scaling\n- Optimization algorithm: SLSQP with bounded variables and equality constraint\n- Repair check: performed after each optimization iteration and before candidate acceptance\n- Default parameters: (minfeval=100, maxfeval=5000, top=5 best candidates per run)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Time budget: 1100 seconds, distributed across multiple n_points and optimizations\n- Memory: n_points <= 1000 samples (storing 2D arrays for correlations)\n- Estimated cost: O(n_points^2) per function evaluation\n- Deadline handling: each optimization run has a 500-second timeout\n- Handling failure: return best candidate from parent or search\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Input validation: check parent solution format and n_points > 0\n- Feasibility checks: enforce 0<=h[i]<=1 and exact sum(h)=n_points/2\n- Objective evaluation: use np.correlate with mode='full' and dx scaling\n- Conversion to steps: parent solution includes interpolation techniques\n- Return requirements: (h_values_final, c5_bound_final, n_points_used)",
